Well, maybe we can come to a synthesis on the issue.  This is my
thinking:

1. Turn-based doesn't necessarily mean not real-time.  The easiest way
(although not necessarily the best) to add real-time play to a
roguelike is to make "rest" moves if the player doesn't act within a
given time threshold.  Interhack, the old, imploded multiplayer Nethack
variation that has long vanished from the web used a technique like
this.  I suspect that Dungeon Hack did too.

2. Danger in Nethack (and most other roguelikes) tends to be at random
hotspot moments surrounded by many turns of relative safety.  A
real-time roguelike would simply demand that those relatively harmless
times be used to prepare for the danger moments.  It'd require some
additional degree of preparation, but not significantly greater than
that already required by the game.

